  "label": "Interpretation of the code-change effect. The diagnostic lam=0 figures below are NOT official control or v05 values; they were computed after the mandated runs, by setting rates.lam=0 on the already-built v04-control cases and calling run_case, to show what the 377f177 identity (b = gt/roict) would have produced on this commit.",
  "observation": "Control (v04 request on fd50e97, default lam=0.25, inflation prompt 0.0224) matches the published v04 figures (said to have been run on 377f177) to about 0.001\u20130.003 per share, i.e. rounding at the third decimal. On 377f177, compile.py and defaults.py had no rates.lam / inflation pass-through, and forecast_capex method 1 used b = gt/roict (lam=0). A diagnostic lam=0 run on the control cases gives 149.779 / 178.555 / 85.129, which would have been the expected 377f177-equivalent levels. Those do not match published v04 (154.497 / 184.150 / 87.854). The published v04 figures instead match this control (default lam=0.25). So the mandated code-change effect (control minus published v04) is negligible, and the published v04 run appears already to have used the post-e27579d identity with default lam=0.25, rather than 377f177 as stated. The lambda effect (v05 lam=1 minus control lam=0.25) is the economically meaningful change.",
